<section class="lp-section" id="comparison"><div class="lp-wrap"><div class="lp-heading"><p class="lp-kicker">01 / Understand the difference</p><h2>Power vs parallel<br>vs predictive dialing.</h2><p>“Auto dialer” is the broad category. The useful question is how the software starts a call and gets a live answer to a person.</p></div>
<div class="sg-table-wrap"><table class="lp-table sg-table" role="table"><caption>Four calling methods: operation, suitable work and the tradeoff</caption><thead><tr role="row"><th scope="col" role="columnheader">Method</th><th scope="col" role="columnheader">What happens</th><th scope="col" role="columnheader">Use it for</th><th scope="col" role="columnheader">The tradeoff</th></tr></thead><tbody>{% for mode in guide.modes %}<tr role="row"><th scope="row" role="rowheader">{{ mode.name }}</th><td role="cell" data-label="What happens">{{ mode.operation }}</td><td role="cell" data-label="Use it for">{{ mode.fit }}</td><td role="cell" data-label="The tradeoff">{{ mode.tradeoff }}<small><strong>Our call:</strong> {{ mode.verdict }}</small></td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody></table></div>
<div class="sg-two sg-after"><div><h3>What is progressive dialing?</h3><p>It generally starts the next call when a caller is available. Some vendors use “power” and “progressive” for similar workflows. Check whether you can pause, read the record and finish notes before the next call starts.</p></div><div><h3>Is parallel the same as predictive?</h3><p>Parallel describes multiple calls happening together. Predictive describes the system adjusting its pace. Products can combine these behaviours. Nooks, for example, now offers adaptive modes that change the number of active lines using pickup predictions.</p></div></div>
<p class="sg-sources">Definitions and product behaviour: <a href="https://www.five9.com/faq/what-is-an-auto-dialer" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Five9 auto-dialer explanation ↗</a> · <a href="https://help.close.com/feature-guide/power-predictive-dialing/using-the-predictive-dialer" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Close predictive documentation ↗</a> · <a href="https://support.nooks.ai/articles/3845888418-adaptive-and-turbo-dialing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nooks adaptive modes ↗</a></p>
</div></section>

<section class="lp-section lp-paper" id="choose-your-mode"><div class="lp-wrap"><div class="lp-heading"><p class="lp-kicker">02 / Start with the work</p><h2>Which dialer fits<br>a small sales team?</h2><p>Count the people calling at the same time. Ten licences with two people logged in still means only two callers.</p></div>
<div class="sg-flow"><div><h3>Solo caller or a short, valuable list</h3><p><strong>Start with preview or single-line power.</strong> Read the account, make the call and decide the next step. This suits owner-led sales, named prospects, switchboards and promised callbacks.</p><p>Buying more lines makes little sense if you need preparation before every conversation.</p></div><div><h3>A prepared queue with long waits</h3><p><strong>Trial parallel with fewer lines first.</strong> Use similar buyer types and calling windows to compare sessions. Watch intended-buyer conversations, follow-up completion and missed human answers.</p><p>If the extra speed creates rushed notes or poor callbacks, reduce it.</p></div><div><h3>Several callers sharing a queue</h3><p><strong>Assess predictive with everyone working together.</strong> The system needs enough available callers to handle the answers it creates. Check how breaks, long calls and wrap-up time change the pace.</p></div></div>
<div class="sg-note"><strong>CallTeam's verdict:</strong> a higher dial count earns nothing by itself. Pay for more useful conversations and completed follow-up. Keep the slower mode available for the accounts that need it.</div>
<div class="sg-two sg-after"><div><h3>Example: a researched account list</h3><p>You have 80 target companies, several contacts per account and a specific reason to call each one. Use a queue that gives the caller time to read the notes and navigate reception. Calling the same switchboard from several records at once creates a problem you could have avoided.</p></div><div><h3>Example: a repeatable prospecting session</h3><p>Four callers have prepared lists, a consistent offer and a clear follow-up process. Ringing takes up much of the session. That is a reasonable setting to compare power with parallel or predictive. Keep the list quality and message comparable while judging the mode.</p></div></div>
<p class="sg-sources">For number selection and screening replies, use our <a href="/resources/how-to-buy-cold-calling-leads/">mobile vs company number guide</a>.</p>
</div></section>

